 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to early decision admissions\ndata for higher education institutions",
    "basePrintNo": "S8118",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8118-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add Title 7 Art 129-C §6450, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8118\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to early decision admis-\nsions data for higher education institutions\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo require colleges who use early decision admissions to report on the\nimpact of such admissions\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of this bill adds a new article 129-c to the Education Law\nto require higher education institutions which offer early decision\nadmissions to report on:\n\n- Deadlines by which prospective students must apply early decision,\nearly action, and/or general admissions to the college, and when they\ncan generally expect to hear back\n\n-The overall acceptance rate for first-year students vs. students admit-\nted early decision, early action, or general admissions, including total\nnumber of applications received - The number of first-year students\nadmitted under each type of admissions plan and percentage that they\nmake up of the first-year class\n\n- The racial and/or ethnic identity of prospective vs. accepted\nstudents, and which type of admissions plan they were accepted under\n\n- How many first-year students received federal Pell grants, disaggre-\ngated by type of admissions plan\n\n- Total and average amounts of financial aid disbursed to students in\neach type of admissions plan\n\n- Whether full financial need is met for students applying early deci-\nsion\n\n- The median and average income of first-year students, disaggregated by\ntype of admissions plan\n\n- What type of high school first-year students attended, and whether it\nis in state, out-of-state, or abroad, disaggregated by type of admis-\nsions plan\n\n- A detailed description of a prospective student's commitment to the\ninstitution upon applying early decision, and what consequences they may\nface if they change their mind after being admitted\n\nThe report shall be published online on the New York State Education\nDepartment's, which regulates colleges through its Office of College and\nUniversity Evaluation, website. The website shall allow a user to view\nthe data for each college included in the report via a dropdown menu.\n\nInstitutions who willfully fail to comply with the provisions of this\nact shall be subject to a civil penalty worth 10% of state aid, which\ntakes different forms depending on if the institution is private or\npublic.\n\nSection two sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMany colleges take advantage of early decision (ED) admissions policies,\nwhich allow prospective students to apply in November of their senior\nyear and receive an expedited decision in exchange for committing to\nattend an institution if accepted, in order to secure a significant\nportion of an upcoming class before the application for general admis-\nsions opens. This can help a college budget to meet financial need, as\nthey will know how much aid they've already dispensed vs. what they need\nto reserve for the rest of the class, as well as what an incoming class\nis likely to look like and thus how to select the rest of the applicants\nto ensure a diverse range of backgrounds and interests. Many colleges\nargue that they partner with pipeline programs to use early decision to\ntarget underrepresented students, who may not otherwise apply, to build\na diverse class early on. ED also allows colleges to lock in institu-\ntional priorities, such as athletes or legacies, at an earlier stage and\nlowers their overall admission rate. Early decision is distinct from\nearly action admissions, which allow students to apply early but do not\nrequire them to commit to attend if accepted.\n\nDespite these advantages, early decision has one glaring flaw: it\nrequires students to commit to attending before they can compare finan-\ncial packages in the spring and thus overwhelmingly benefits students\nfrom wealthy backgrounds. Providing a significant admissions advantage,\nwith some studies showing that applying ED is worth an extra 100 points\non the SAT, at the most elite colleges up to 50% of an incoming class is\nadmitted early decision. At Brown and Duke University, applying ED makes\nan applicant four times more likely to be admitted than if they were to\napply regular decision. At Dartmouth College and Columbia University, an\napplicant is three times more likely.\n\nA closer look at who exactly benefits from ED, meanwhile, reveals a\nportrait of stark class and income disparity. Almost a third of students\nfrom independent high schools applied ED, more than 3.5 times the\npercentage of public school students. International applicants are\nalmost three times more likely to apply ED than US residents, and appli-\ncants from the country's wealthiest ZIP codes are twice as likely to\napply ED than other applicants. One study found that the single strong-\nest predictor of whether a not a student was likely to apply ED was\nwhether they had hired a college admissions consultant, and the share of\nBlack students who apply ED is almost half of those in the regular\nadmissions pool. While some schools commit to meeting the full financial\nneed of the ED applicants they accept, most do not. And while colleges\nwill claim that students can back out of an ED offer and incur no finan-\ncial penalty, the fact remains that the majority of students and parents\nare largely unaware of this exception - and the schools, for obvious\nreasons, are loath to advertise it.\n\nThis bill requires all colleges in New York who use early decision\nadmissions to report on how much of their class they are filling from\nthe ED vs. regular admissions pool, acceptance rates for ED vs.  regular\nadmissions, the aggregate Pell status and median incomes of ED vs.\ngeneral admissions students, and average financial aid disbursed for\nboth pools. While the US Department of Education has the ability to\ncollect and disaggregate admissions data such as this across a range of\ncategories, it does not currently do so. And while the Common Data Set\nsurvey, meanwhile, which is an attempt by the College Board, Peterson's,\nand the U.S. News & World Report to get colleges to report on a wealth\nof admissions information, contains a category on early decisions,\nseveral notable institutions leave it conspicuously blank.\n\nIn a world in which selective colleges fill more than half their first-\nyear classes by early January, the average student may have virtually no\nshot at being admitted to some of our state's most celebrated insti-\ntutions. A snapshot into exactly which types of students are applying ED\nand how colleges use it to shape their classes will be an invaluable\ntool for policymakers and the public.  It will spur important debates\nabout who benefits from the status quo, who is left behind, and how we\ncan design a better system to uplift all students in the college admis-\nsions process.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law and shall apply to academic years commencing on and after\nsuch date.\n",
